# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 solve.cc: inside solve, epsilon is 10^-Digits, and the MPFR evalf of a real root written as a rootof (the real root of a cubic with one irrational real root) carries a rounding imaginary part of that size (x^3-x-1 at Digits 40: +0.163E-39*i), so solve_ckrange's is_zero(im) dropped it as a complex root: solve(x^3-x-1=0,x) = [] at Digits 40, solve(x^3-2*x-5=0,x) = [] at 60 (right at 12). Now the three candidates of the cubic branch are decided by the exact sign of the discriminant (one real root: the candidate with the smallest imaginary part, evaluated in MPFR at a precision doubled until the conjugate pair separates: the candidates are rootofs of a degree-6 polynomial, ill-conditioned when two roots are close, and as doubles x^3-3*x+2+10^-22 gave three roots at Digits 12, two of them no roots), and an MPFR candidate elsewhere is real when |im| <= |sol|*10^(2-Digits). solve_inequation had the same epsilon in three places: the double test point between two roots stayed complex (the interval was skipped: solve(x^3-2*x-5>0,x) = []), e at a root was not 0 within epsilon (> became >= at Digits 60), and a root whose e had the wrong sign by rounding was added to a strict inequality as an isolated point (solve(x^3-x-1<0,x) = [x<r, r] at Digits 12).
